    "result_text": "Build succeeds and all tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nRedesigned owner identity so `customerCode` is `<REGION>-<HASH8>` and every value derived from it follows.\n\n**Identity formatting \u2014 `CustomerCode`** (now `public`)\n- `format(region, normalizedTelephone, lastName)` \u2192 `region + \"-\" + HASH8`, where `HASH8` is the first 8 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)`.\n- Added `region(customerCode)` to read the `<REGION>` segment back. Dropped the old `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` sequence format entirely.\n\n**Assignment \u2014 `AssignCustomerCode`**\n- Now derives the region from the owner's postcode via `CityRegion.localityOf(postcode, city)` and hashes the already-normalized telephone + last name. No more per-city counting, so it no longer needs `OwnerRepository`/`Cities` \u2014 the code is a pure function of the owner's own fields.\n\n**Shared hashing \u2014 new `Hashes` helper**\n- Extracted `SHA-256 \u2192 upper-case hex prefix` into one package-private util. Both `CustomerCode` (8 chars) and `Households.id` (12 chars) now use it, removing the digest code that was duplicated in `Households`.\n\n**Locality \u2014 `OwnerMapper.locality`**\n- Now sourced from the identity: it returns `CustomerCode.region(owner.getCustomerCode())`, falling back to the postcode/city derivation only for legacy owners with no code (seed rows have `customer_code` NULL).\n\n**Membership number, check digit, audit** \u2014 already built from `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so they moved to the new identity automatically once the code format changed. Verified in the audit line: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash` passes (`NSW-\u2026` matches `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`), and the full suite is green.",
